    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the concept.\n\n**RBAC** is an authorization topic. The UI may hide or disable features, but the authoritative allow/deny decision must be enforced at the trusted API or service boundary.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the asset, attacker capability, trust boundary, secure default, enforcement point, and failure response before writing code.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core decision or policy check so you can explain the security reasoning line by line.\n\n```ts\ninterface Principal {\n  roles: string[];\n}\n\nfunction can(\n  principal: Principal,\n  requiredRole: string,\n): boolean {\n  for (let i = 0; i < principal.roles.length; i += 1) {\n    if (principal.roles[i] === requiredRole) return true;\n  }\n\n  return false;\n}\n\nconst allowed = can(\n  { roles: [\"admin\", \"support\"] },\n  \"admin\",\n);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or browser APIs when they make the same security decision clearer without hiding the important control.\n\n```ts\nconst principal = {\n  roles: new Set([\"admin\", \"support\"]),\n};\n\nconst allowed =\n  principal.roles.has(\"admin\");\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse RBAC to express a small, auditable policy contract. The client can improve UX, but every protected operation must be re-authorized by the trusted service.\n\nStep 6 — Validation:\nTest the allowed path, denied path, malformed input, missing credential, expired state, and misconfiguration that could broaden access.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain what is trusted, what is untrusted, where the final enforcement happens, what the secure default is, and how the control is monitored and migrated.",
    productionExample: "Use RBAC to express a small, auditable policy contract. The client can improve UX, but every protected operation must be re-authorized by the trusted service.",
